{"author":{"id":"038d65070aa747b6bc9d50939fa697ec871a40ed259db54429b255bbef2581d6"},"ops":[{"type":3,"timestamp":1792968433,"nonce":"0o1q0WReRaaNWgPcX/pwg/e6AkU=","message":"Implemented and verified in the working tree. The browser editor now uses a session-process stdio LSP adapter. It resolves executables, cwd and environment on the workspace host; framing, pipe I/O, JSON decoding and process startup run on workers with ash.Future waits. Ready servers sleep until stream events wake the window. Input/output queues, Content-Length headers and message sizes are bounded, including in-flight data. Rust session stdin and socket outboxes are bounded too; slow consumers close their connection and release owned programs.\n\nNetwork loss pauses both extension and LSP supervision without spending their restart budgets. Reconnect restarts previously active hosts and replays current unsaved buffers. Explicit Stop survives reconnect and extension re-registration. Connection generations fence spawn, write, stdin-close and kill at both the Haxe caller and browser forwarding boundary, so stale handles and late spawn replies cannot affect a replacement PID. UI session listeners drain only at the frame boundary; program workers and extension pending checks no longer dispatch UI listeners.\n\nThe full browser outage test exposed a second failure: removing the paused LSP registration invoked the native Haxe inspection fallback. CompilerServer scanned localhost ports from WASM, creating enough worker WebSockets to prevent the actual session reconnecting. Compiler socket probing is now disabled for browser/remote-session inspection. Remote Run uses a plain session-hosted Haxe invocation instead of the editor's local compiler socket. Unnecessary Explorer rescans on language-server lifecycle changes were also removed.\n\nBrowser hover documentation now parses Markdown locally through a bounded inert-template conversion to MarkdownView data, with portable code highlighting and no native rayscript Markdown primitive. View callback failures are logged without restarting a healthy language server. The pinned marked production dependency's MIT license is staged with the web artifact.\n\nValidation passed:\n- SessionLspChecks against a real Rust session and actual Rayzor: 16 concurrent semantic hovers; framed Unicode and large messages, cwd/env, cancellation, request/callback isolation, ANSI stderr, versioned diagnostics, idle wake, restart and cleanup. Actual session-hosted Haxe Run also compiled and executed successfully without localhost probes.\n- SessionLspQueueChecks and SessionLspReconnectChecks: bounded queues, cancellation during spawn, overflow cleanup, offline replay, explicit Stop, idle Welcome, stale handles and late spawn replies with reused PIDs.\n- Existing native LspChecks, ProjectRunnerChecks, ExtensionManagerChecks and SessionAsyncChecks.\n- Rust session: 5 unit and 16 integration tests. Browser session package: 23 tests. Production npm audit: zero advisories.\n- tests/web/lsp.mjs: full WASM editor and built-in Rayzor extension, real hover/Markdown/diagnostics, sustained gateway/backend interruption, offline edits replayed, live diagnostics after recovery, and zero native compiler socket probes.\n- Full two-editor collaboration regression: author-scoped undo, save failure, reconnect, tab lifecycle and reload. Public landing demo regression also passed without private API calls.\n\nVerified artifact staged atomically to bin/web/rayde (assets first, WASM and entry page last). Detailed results/captures are under bin/web/lsp-shots, bin/session-lsp-*.log and bin/web/lsp-*-regression.log. Changes remain uncommitted; no deployment or push. The parent stays open for compiler-front-end-in-WASM work. Durable Object document authority, PTYs and the trusted MSB provider remain in their existing tracks.","files":null}]}